E2E/KeyRest.lean: THE LETTERS OF THE KEY FILE's THREE THREADS THAT THE TESTS WITH CODE DO NOT READ
(`Rest`, E2E/KeyThreadBase.lean): AN HONEST FILLING OUT OF THE LIBRARY's OWN TEXTS. What an item
says is the library's meaning of the key file's table of joints, at the key file's own states,
turned at two items as the threads of the proof file turn it. What holds of the state at a border is
THE STATE's SIDE OF THE LIBRARY's PROMISE there (its promise at a border: what its table of joints
asks to hold there beside the open items: a Bool on the state, ties that are no Bool, and facts on
the oracles: `on₁`, `on₂`, `on₃`; at the shared chain the library has sorted it into three heaps, of
which `Tests₂` is the one on the states alone). What a step keeps is an equation on the whole state.
Definitions; and statements that try them. NOTHING IS SHOWN OF ANY PROVER: no completeness, nothing
on what a view shows.

WHAT IS CALLED. `KItem.holds`, `KMeaning` (Johnson/Desc/Key/KeyBorders.lean) · `meaning₁At`,
`plan₁`, `readers₁`, `book₁`, `shape₁` (Johnson/RunOne/KeyRows1At.lean, KeyReaders1.lean) ·
`meaning₂`, `meaning₃`, `shapeB₃` (Johnson/RunOne/KeyRows23At.lean) · `Tests₂`
(Johnson/RunOne/KeyOn2Parts.lean) · `last₁At` (Johnson/RunOne/KeySeam1.lean) · `BaseSt` with its
three readings `h₁`, `h₂`, `h₃` (Johnson/RunOne/KeyOneReading.lean) · the seats `atSix`
(Johnson/RunOne/KeyFillBaseAt.lean) and the numbers `off₂N`, `off₃N`
(Johnson/Top/Fire/Code/KeyAtOff.lean) · the last chain's letters as the library's own promise there
has them, and `AtFk` (Johnson/RunOne/KeyRowsAtFk.lean) · `readsL_h₁` (Johnson/RunOne/KeyReadsL.lean)
· `Wit`, `Reading`, `msgOf`, `PIN` (Johnson/Desc/Msg.lean) · `eq₁`, `eq₂`, `eq₃`
(E2E/KeyKeepsEq.lean) · `Side18`, `Side₂`, `Side₃`, `KeyFits` (E2E/KeySideDef.lean).

  `KeyFill`: THE LETTERS THAT STAY, in one structure: how a witness is read · ONE `BaseSt` for the
      three chains' readings · THE SCRIPT (the cells; six messages, each a function of ITS OWN
      CHAIN's challenges, with the number of these after which it turns no more; the pads) · the two
      earlier chains' last states, for the last chain · what the step of a KIND's sub-protocol keeps
      · of which inputs something is said · the trees' tables
  `one₀`: THE PUBLIC VALUES ARE NO LETTER: they are those that the description's own first chain is
      made of. NOR ARE THE SHAPE OF THE LATER CHAINS (`shapeAt`: one term), THE SEATS, THE BOOKS'
      NUMBERS, THE NUMBERS OF TREES THAT THE FIRST TWO CHAINS SEND (6 and 11) AND THE FOUR LETTERS
      OF THE LAST CHAIN's BOOL: they are the library's
  `KeyFill.wit`, `KeyFill.witLast`: THE WITNESS, of the first chain's challenges and the shared
      chain's. The three threads hear unlike lists: the first its own; the shared chain's its own
      alone; the last the first's, then the shared chain's, then its own. So each thread hands the
      witness its two lists in its own way; the last cuts its one list at THE NUMBER OF THE FIRST
      CHAIN's DECLARED ROUNDS, off the description: no letter
  **`KeyFill.wit_first`**, **`KeyFill.wit_shared`**: ONE MESSAGE ACROSS THE SEAMS: a message of the
      first chain is the same in the first and in the last thread; a message of the shared chain the
      same in the shared and in the last thread, once it turns no more. (The terms part at the other
      chain's messages, of which a thread says nothing.)
  **`holdsH`**: WHAT AN ITEM SAYS ON THE HONEST SIDE: the library's sentence, but: a pin says that
      the state holds the seat's sample and the witness's message meets it, WITHOUT "an encoding of
      it is near the committed word"; the pin on the stored table says that the carried OOD sample
      is the cells' own, WITHOUT the decoder
  `Own₂`, `Handed₂`, **`tests₂_iff`**: the library's facts on the state alone at a border of the
      shared chain, CUT IN TWO: what reads the key and the chain's own state; what reads the first
      chain's last state. The library's heap is the Bool, the one and the other
  **`rest₁`**, **`rest₂`**, **`rest₃`**: THE FILLING: a family in the index, the committer's coins
      and the instance
  `LettersOk`, `LettersOk.blind`, `LettersOk.readsL`: A PREMISE's FORM: what ties the letters to
      each other and to the plan: THE LIBRARY's OWN LIST OF WHAT IS LEFT AS A PREMISE (`AtFk`) and
      what it lacks. Under it two premises of other statements hold
  `filled₁`, `filled₂`, `filled₃`: the three threads so filled, over the base threads: WITHOUT
      HALVES: these are laid by `seat₁`, `seat₂`, `seat₃` (E2E/KeyLaid.lean)
  **`laid₁`**, **`laid₂`**, **`laid₃`**: THE SAME WITH ANY HALVES AND ANY TABLES: what is shown of
      the verifier's side is shown at these
  `Encodes`: A DEBT WITH A NAME: the committed word at message k's seat IS an encoding of the
      witness's message. **`pin_of_honest`**: with it, where every leaf is shown, the honest pin
      gives the library's `PIN`
  `sharedItems`, `shared_listed`, `shared_pins`: the fourteen items that the shared chain's thread
      bears; it bears no other; the pins open at its borders are those of its own three messages: by
      evaluation
  `BlindG`, **`shared_blind`**, `means₂_iff`: THE SHARED THREAD's SENTENCES DO NOT READ THE FIRST
      CHAIN's STATE, where the reading's sample at the three seats and the OOD sample do not: so "at
      every such state" asks no more than "at one"
  **`blind_base`**: THAT PREMISE IS MET BY THE LIBRARY's OWN `BaseSt.h₂`, where the seats of the
      shared chain's three messages are theirs

THE CHOICES. (1) The shared thread is handed the table's key, which its input holds: the key file's
instance IS that key. The library's meaning there is handed the instance WITH THE FIRST CHAIN's LAST
STATE, which this thread is not: each item is said at every such state · (2) the two pins as above ·
(3) THE TREES' TABLES ARE A PARAMETER HERE: this file does not fix them. At the tables of the play
(Johnson/Desc/ProofFile/Honest/Small/PlayedTo.lean) `Encodes` is a premise of its own · (4)
`valid` names the whole input · (5) THE CLAUSE ON THE STATE IS THE STATE's SIDE OF THE LIBRARY's
PROMISE, AND NOTHING THAT READS AN ORACLE. AT THE FIRST CHAIN: the clause with code is the library's
Bool `shape₁`; beside it `Side18` (of which that Bool is the first part) and "the stored table's OOD
sample is there". AT THE SHARED CHAIN: THE CLAUSE WITH CODE IS IDLE (`true`); beside it `Side₂`: the
library's whole heap `Tests₂`, ITS BOOL AMONG IT, at every good state of the first chain; and "the
OOD sample is there". So out of this thread alone neither that Bool nor `Own₂` can be had: they come
out under one closed public premise, or where a good state of the first chain is in hand
(E2E/KeySideCash.lean). AT THE LAST CHAIN: the clause with code is the library's Bool `shapeB₃`;
beside it `Side₃`: the library's heap on that chain's own state (of which that Bool is the first
part), and that the codes' lengths are in order. THE SAMPLES AT THE OPEN PINS STAND IN NO CLAUSE:
the library's Bool says that the message at an open pin is in the statement, sampled, with a sample
row (E2E/KeySampledAt.lean); that the READING returns a sample there is said by the pin's own
sentence, and is owed where a pin is owed: at the library's own filling it asks more than the Bool
holds, among it that a drawn factor reads as a unit (`rowIsK`, Johnson/RunOne/KeyFillSeed.lean): a
fact on the play. DROPPED EVERYWHERE: "at most one message of the list meets the sample", which is
an event of a run's coins, and the promise's first arm ("or the state is marked"), which is never
taken at these readers · (6) nothing rides · (7) what a step keeps: at the sixteen sub-protocols of
no kind an equation on the whole state, by text; at a kind's a letter.

Limits.
- DEFINITIONS. Nothing shows that a thread so filled can hold at any run, that the script's messages
  are what the parts send, or that a debt at a sub-protocol can be paid. What is shown of the clause
  on the state: E2E/KeyMade.lean (from the verifier's own fold), KeyBehind.lean, KeyBehind3.lean
  (behind a step; `side₃_of_threads`: `Side₃` at the last chain's start).
- THE LIBRARY's TIES THAT ARE NO BOOL (its `free₁` and the like), whole, ARE NOT THE CLAUSE AT ANY
  CHAIN: they ask besides what stands in `LettersOk` and that some oracles show every leaf at the
  open pins: whoever calls a law of the library's that takes them must make them from the clause:
  not typed.
- THAT THE KEY FITS THE SHAPE's INDEX (`KeyFits`) STANDS IN NO THREAD: it is a plain premise of what
  is shown of the shared chain's state from its start on (E2E/KeyStateSide2.lean, KeyMade.lean);
  behind a step nothing is asked of the instance (E2E/KeyBehind.lean).
- WHERE NO GOOD STATE OF THE FIRST CHAIN EXISTS THE SHARED THREAD's CLAUSE ON THE STATE HOLDS OF
  EVERYTHING.
- ONE MESSAGE IS NOT ONE SENTENCE: that the pin and the sample rows of the shared chain's second
  message say in the last thread what they said in the shared one wants besides that the two
  readings agree at the seam, that the rows read what the pin pins there, and that the message turns
  no more: no such statement is typed.
- THE LIBRARY SAYS OF ITS SIX SEATS AND OF THE NUMERALS 6 AND 11 THAT THEY ARE TYPED BY HAND AND ARE
  THE FULL SIZE's (at its small example the second is 8), and of the last arm of the last chain's
  numbers that it is shown nowhere. THE THREADS ARE TYPED AT EVERY DESCRIPTION AND CAN HOLD AT THE
  FULL SIZE ALONE.
- THE LAST THREAD IS HANDED THE TWO VALUES OF THE LETTER `ends`: a bare letter: that they are the
  states at which the two earlier threads end is not shown. Its clause on how the state was made,
  and its items' sentences, read them.
- The first chain's meaning speaks at the shape inside the public values (`one₀`), the later chains'
  at `shapeAt`: equal under the description's own test on the file, not by unfolding.
- The structure's fields are letters: `kind₁`, `kind₂`, `kind₃`, `meetsT`, `ends`, all nine fields
  of `base`, the script and the tables have no text here. `enc` and `rad` of the structure are other
  letters than `base`'s of these names.
- FILLINGS AT WHICH ALL IS IDLE: `meetsT` or `Rel` false everywhere · a `BaseSt` without the stored
  table's OOD sample, or without samples (then no pin's sentence holds) · `kind₁`, `kind₂` or
  `kind₃` false · `ends` outside what the last chain's start accepts · `fixedMsg n` beyond what is
  heard where an item on message n is owed: an item is said at every continuation of the challenges.
- THE NUMERALS 6 AND 11 ARE TIED TO NOTHING HERE: that the first chain states six trees and the
  shared chain eleven are public facts in scope, which the library has; they stand in no clause and
  no lemma here.
- The library's files on the table say that no row is confirmed.

-/
